module gunbc.v1_complexity_capability_census



data v1_complexity_capability_census_note: String = "C0(b1) of the v2 complexity capability parity program: a REVIEWED capability roster over src/v1/complexity.dag, with every citation carried as a typed DeclarationRef that resolves through the single resolution authority rather than as a name matched by substring. It is deliberately NOT the closed capability denominator — that is C0(b2), the declaration-to-capability totality slice, which derives the population from DeclFact instead of authoring it. This module states the difference rather than blurring it (operator review, 2026-08-05): what is proven here is that the authored rows are well-formed, exactly cited, and disposition-partitioned; what is NOT proven here is that no capability is missing."

data c0_scope_note: String = "Scope, stated so the title cannot overclaim. PROVEN by this carrier and its witnesses: every row carries a typed DeclarationRef; every ref resolves to exactly one declaration through v2.std.decl_ref_resolution (long lane, which pays the decl_facts scan); capability identity is a closed coproduct, so an identity OUTSIDE the vocabulary is unrepresentable — but the roster is a List, so the same identity CAN appear twice and distinctness is VALIDATED by an executing witness, not constructed (claim narrowed on operator review 2026-08-05: the earlier 'duplicates are unrepresentable' was too strong). C0(b2)'s derived declaration population supplies the construction wall this list cannot FOR DISTINCTNESS AND FOR DECLARATION COVERAGE, though not for capability discovery; every disposition arm lands in exactly one work-state partition, with a controlled fixture for every arm including the uninhabited ones. NOT PROVEN: declaration-grain completeness. A 26th semantic capability omitted from this roster would not be detected here — AND, corrected 2026-08-05 against the b2 author own report, it is NOT detected by C0(b2) either. b2 derives the DECLARATION population from DeclFact and classifies each declaration against this closed ComplexityCapabilityId vocabulary; a capability absent from the vocabulary therefore has no row to fail against and no declaration classifies to it. The earlier wording said such a capability would not be detected HERE, which implied b2 was its trigger. It is not. What would actually close it is capability discovery FROM THE SURFACE — deriving the capability set from what v1.compiler.complexity does rather than checking an authored set against declarations — and no slice of this program currently owns that. Naming it as an untriggered stall is the point (DESIGN §4b meta-obligation 2: separate cannot-climb from can-climb-after-one-grounding from can-climb-now-but-unbuilt; this one is the third, and it had a wrong trigger attached)."

data v1_declaration_counting_method_note: String = "Method identity for the declaration count, which two notes reported differently (operator review, 2026-08-05). Over src/v1/complexity.dag at this revision: 171 counts top-level `fn` declarations only; 201 counts `fn` + `type` + `data` + `let`; 26 are `type` and 4 are `data`. Both figures are correct under their own method and neither was wrong — what was missing is the method, without which the two numbers read as a contradiction. This note is the method authority; a figure quoted without one is not quotable. C0(b2) derives its denominator from DeclFact rather than from either grep, at which point this note is superseded by the derived population."

data c0_authority_ruling_note: String = "C0(a) AUTHORITY RULING — SIGNED by the operator 2026-08-05. The semantic split below is approved and no sibling cost taxonomy may be created. v2.lens.cost is the single migration authority for cost and size expressions, discrete work, work/span, output-size and peak-space expressions, and semantic effect demand. v2.lens.complexity carries asymptotic projection and complexity-class comparison, and nothing else. std.realization_schedule carries physical and resource projections and scheduling accounts. v1.compiler.complexity is the legacy oracle, parity source, and deletion target. NUANCE CARRIED WITH THE SIGNATURE: v2.lens.cost is the authority's CURRENT IDENTITY, not necessarily its permanent namespace — its WallNow classification already raises whether a construction should live under v2.lens.*, and any later move to a shared or non-lens home must be an ATOMIC RELOCATION with every consumer repointed in the same motion. A second live authority is never an acceptable intermediate state. The ruling is carried by THREE typed axes below, not one: CostProgramRole says what each module IS (including the legacy oracle), CostMigrationDestination says where owed work may LAND (the legacy oracle absent by construction, so migrating into the deletion target is unrepresentable), and CapabilityFactProducer says WHO produces the facts. An earlier revision fused all three into a single CostAuthorityModule enum, which made 'migrate into v1.compiler.complexity' writable and forced parser-progress to name the cost lens as its producer against its own explanation (operator review 2026-08-05)."

type CostProgramRole
  = RoleMigrationAuthority
  | RoleAsymptoticProjection
  | RoleRealizationAccounts
  | RoleLegacyOracle

data cost_program_role_note: String = "The four ROLES the signed C0(a) ruling assigns. This axis says what a module IS in the program, and it deliberately includes the legacy oracle, because naming v1.compiler.complexity's role is part of the ruling. It is NOT the migration-destination axis: the two were one enum in the previous revision, which made 'migrate into the legacy oracle' a writable row (operator review 2026-08-05). Splitting them makes that state unrepresentable rather than merely unlikely."

fn cost_program_role_module_path(r: CostProgramRole) -> String {
  match r {
    RoleMigrationAuthority => "v2.lens.cost"
    RoleAsymptoticProjection => "v2.lens.complexity"
    RoleRealizationAccounts => "std.realization_schedule"
    RoleLegacyOracle => "v1.compiler.complexity"
  }
}

type CostMigrationDestination
  = MigrateToV2LensCost
  | MigrateToV2LensComplexity
  | MigrateToStdRealizationSchedule

data cost_migration_destination_note: String = "Where owed work LANDS. The legacy oracle is absent by construction: v1.compiler.complexity is the deletion target, so a row routing migration INTO it is not a mistake to catch but a state with no constructor. This is the §5 move the previous revision missed by reusing one enum for role and destination."

fn cost_migration_destination_module_path(d: CostMigrationDestination) -> String {
  match d {
    MigrateToV2LensCost => "v2.lens.cost"
    MigrateToV2LensComplexity => "v2.lens.complexity"
    MigrateToStdRealizationSchedule => "std.realization_schedule"
  }
}

type CapabilityFactProducer
  = FactsFromCostLens
  | FactsFromV2Parser
  | FactsFromSharedSubstrate

data capability_fact_producer_note: String = "WHO produces the facts, which is a third axis the previous revision fused into destination. The live specimen that forced the split: parser-progress facts are produced by the v2 PARSER and joined through std.termination, not computed by the cost lens — yet with one enum the row had to name v2.lens.cost as though the lens produced them, contradicting the row's own explanation. Producer and destination are now independent, so a capability whose facts come from elsewhere can still land its accounting in the cost authority."

type ParityPhase
  = ParityC1ExpressionAuthority
  | ParityC2SummaryRestored
  | ParityC4Interprocedural
  | ParityC5RealizationBridge
  | ParityC6LiveConsumers
  | ParityC7ParityCorpus
  | DiscreteCostC1Valuation

data parity_phase_note: String = "The closed phase vocabulary both design notes already declare. A migration row names the phase that owns it from this set, so 'which slice does this land in' is a typed edge rather than a sentence that can drift from the note it refers to. DiscreteCostC1Valuation is the one phase owned by the sibling note; it appears here because one capability genuinely lands there, and naming it explicitly is what keeps the two notes' scope seam visible instead of implicit."

type ComplexityCapabilityId
  = AsymptoticClassAndDominance
  | PolynomialDegreeReading
  | SizeExpressionAlgebra
  | CostExpressionAlgebra
  | BoundedSummation
  | RetainedMaxExpression
  | WorkAndSpan
  | OutputSizeDerivation
  | PeakSpaceDerivation
  | SpaceTimeDuality
  | CertaintyAxis
  | ConcreteInputEvaluation
  | InterproceduralSummaryMemo
  | CallGraphAndScc
  | DescentEvidenceVocabulary
  | CostBoundVocabulary
  | LoweringTargetVocabulary
  | DescentDerivationWalkers
  | RecursionPatternClassification
  | ParserProgressFactProduction
  | CollectionMethodCostShapes
  | StructuralBoundReport
  | CostExpressionRendering
  | SizeVariableLegend
  | CostAccountSpaceBridge

data complexity_capability_id_note: String = "Capability identity as a closed coproduct rather than a string key. What this buys is exact: an identity OUTSIDE the vocabulary is unrepresentable, and cross-capability edges get a checkable target rather than prose — RetirementOwed names the capability that supersedes it as a variant, not a spelling. What it does NOT buy is distinctness: a List can carry the same variant twice, so inventory_identity_keys_distinct VALIDATES that and says so (operator review 2026-08-05 corrected the earlier overclaim).  C0(b2) joins this set against the derived declaration population — which proves every capability here HAS an authority declaration, and does NOT prove that every capability v1.compiler.complexity implements HAS a row. Those are different claims and only the first is b2 scope. The set remains authored, and its completeness over the surface is unproven by b1 and by b2 alike."

type V1ComplexityResidueId
  = SummaryEvictionHazard
  | ParserProgressV1WalkerRealization

data v1_complexity_residue_note: String = "Legacy residues are NOT semantic capabilities and must not inflate the capability population as peer variants (operator review 2026-08-05). A hazard is a defective realization of a capability that survives beside it; a legacy realization is an implementation the program retires once its replacement executes. Both belong to a capability rather than standing next to one, which is exactly the distinction C0(b2)'s declaration classification needs when it assigns AuthorityOf / HelperOf / LegacyHazardOf / LegacyRealizationOf."

type V1ComplexityInventoryItem
  = SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: ComplexityCapabilityId }
  | LegacyHazardItem { hazard: V1ComplexityResidueId, of_capability: ComplexityCapabilityId }
  | LegacyRealizationItem { realization: V1ComplexityResidueId, of_capability: ComplexityCapabilityId }

data v1_complexity_inventory_item_note: String = "One inventory row is one of three things, and the two residue arms carry the capability they belong to as a typed edge — so a hazard or a legacy realization can never float free of the capability it qualifies. The semantic capability population is exactly the SemanticCapabilityItem rows; counting inventory rows and counting capabilities are now different questions with different answers, which is the honesty the previous revision lacked when SummaryEviction and ParserProgressV1Walker sat as peers beside real capabilities."

type CapabilityRetirementTrigger
  = RetiresWhenReplacementExecutes { replacement: ComplexityCapabilityId }
  | RetiresWithSeedDeletion

data disposition_states_note: String = "Six arms across two work-states, corrected from the previous revision on two operator findings. FIRST: AvailableInSharedSubstrate previously projected as DISCHARGED, which understated C4 — the census's own finding is that v2 has not consumed std.graph / std.termination / std.computation / std.induction, so the work changed from migration to INTEGRATION but did not disappear. It is now IntegrationOwed and projects as owed. SECOND: DeliberatelyRetired previously belonged to none of the three partitions, so the exhaustiveness witness was green only because no row inhabited that arm — green-by-vacancy, which is the inert-check class this repository names. Retirement now splits by whether work remains: RetirementOwed (the replacement has not executed yet, so retiring now would delete a live capability) versus Retired (the obligation is discharged). Every arm, inhabited or not, carries a controlled fixture in the witness."

type ComplexityCapabilityDisposition
  = CompleteInV2 { carrier: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef, note: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | IntegrationOwed { substrate_carrier: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef, phase: ParityPhase, note: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | MigrationOwed { destination: CostMigrationDestination, producer: CapabilityFactProducer, phase: ParityPhase, note: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | SupersededByBetterModel { superseded_by: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef, note: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | RetirementOwed { trigger: CapabilityRetirementTrigger, note: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | Retired { reason: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

data disposition_construction_note: String = "Every arm's routing metadata is a typed identity, not a non-empty string. A destination is a CostMigrationDestination drawn from the signed ruling (the legacy oracle is absent from that vocabulary, so a row migrating INTO v1.compiler.complexity cannot be written); a phase is a ParityPhase; a retirement trigger names the superseding capability as a ComplexityCapabilityId; a carrier is a DeclarationRef that resolves through v2.std.decl_ref_resolution. The previous revision claimed non-empty payloads made an unjustified disposition unwritable — the operator's correction is exact: a non-empty string proves only that someone typed something. What is unwritable now is a row routed to a module the ruling never named, or to a phase that does not exist, or citing a declaration that does not resolve."

type V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: V1ComplexityInventoryItem
  v1_carrier: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef
  disposition: ComplexityCapabilityDisposition
}

fn v1_ref(decl_name: String) -> std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.complexity", decl_name: decl_name)
}

data asymptotic_class_and_dominance: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: AsymptoticClassAndDominance },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "classify_complexity"),
  disposition: CompleteInV2 {
    carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.lens.cost", decl_name: "asymptotic_class_of_cost"),
    note: "v2 carries AsymptoticClass with asymptotic_class_dominates and a bounded lattice — the parity note's own table calls this row better factored than v1's String-returning classifier. No work owed." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data polynomial_degree_reading: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: PolynomialDegreeReading },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "cost_expr_degree"),
  disposition: CompleteInV2 {
    carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.lens.cost", decl_name: "add_polynomial_degree"),
    note: "Audited by reading the composition path, not by matching names: IterativeCost composes as ProductCost, and asymptotic_class_of_cost routes ProductCost through multiply_classes to add_polynomial_degree, so nested iteration does climb degree structurally in v2. The exact per-element reading needs the binder that BoundedSummation carries and is rostered there." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data size_expression_algebra: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: SizeExpressionAlgebra },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "SizeExpr"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC1ExpressionAuthority,
    note: "v1 carries SizeConst/SizeVar/SizeLen/SizeAdd/SizeMax; v2 carries only SizeVariable { source: Node } with no algebra over it. Per parity section 6 the String-keyed SizeVar and SizeLen re-ground onto exact identities rather than carrying their String leaves across." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data cost_expression_algebra: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: CostExpressionAlgebra },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "CostExpr"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC1ExpressionAuthority,
    note: "Verified against both carriers: v2's SymbolicCost has no Max constructor, no binder-carrying Sum, and no extern arm. Per the signed ruling SymbolicCost stays as the normalized asymptotic projection of the richer CostExpr — one authority at two levels of detail, never two competing cost types. Operator ruling 2026-08-05: this migration belongs to parity C1, not to the generic seed-shrink lane; seed deletion CONSUMES the migrated authority, it does not decide its semantics." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data bounded_summation: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: BoundedSummation },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "cost_loop"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC1ExpressionAuthority,
    note: "CostSum { binder, upper, body } expresses the element-dependent fold body, which v2's SumCost { lhs, rhs } cannot represent because it carries no binder. This is the shape an exact per-element count needs and the sibling note names it a prerequisite for its own C2." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data retained_max_expression: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: RetainedMaxExpression },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "cost_conditional"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC1ExpressionAuthority,
    note: "v2's symbolic_max is a lattice join that collapses eagerly to the dominating arm; v1's CostMax is retained. The distinction is load-bearing for exact-at-input evaluation: an eagerly collapsed max has already discarded the arm a known condition would select, so a retained Max is what makes charging only the taken arm expressible without changing the existing fold's meaning for current consumers." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data work_and_span: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: WorkAndSpan },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "cost_par"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC2SummaryRestored,
    note: "v1 carries both axes with cost_seq and cost_par as composition laws; v2 carries neither. Operator ruling 2026-08-05: span stays in C2 and is NOT deferred — deferring it would create an incomplete summary immediately and would prevent derived and measured parallel composition from agreeing, since std.realization_measurement already models that asymmetry on the measured side." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data output_size_derivation: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: OutputSizeDerivation },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "collection_output"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC2SummaryRestored,
    note: "v1's output_size is Map<String, CostExpr> keyed by an output name with a single 'result' convention; the String key re-grounds onto a modeled output identity. Deriving how large a result is as a function of input size has no v2 counterpart at all." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data peak_space_derivation: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: PeakSpaceDerivation },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "derive_peak_space"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC2SummaryRestored,
    note: "peak_space is space_of(work) plus the retained output term, set only at the finalized summary. v1 leaves it absent on intermediate and error summaries — a counted unknown-space frontier rather than a fabricated bound — and that fail-closed posture is what the migrated form must preserve." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data space_time_duality: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: SpaceTimeDuality },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "space_of"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC2SummaryRestored,
    note: "The dual that makes peak space derivable from work rather than separately modeled: sequential steps release so time's sum becomes space's max, concurrent steps co-reside so time's max becomes space's sum, and a fold's iterations release so the summation collapses to its body's peak. Migrating peak space without the dual would re-derive space independently, which is the redundancy the dual removes." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data certainty_axis: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: CertaintyAxis },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "Certainty"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC2SummaryRestored,
    note: "Recording whether a derived bound is proven or conservative is genuinely needed and absent from v2. Its FORM is constrained: parity section 6 rules that it reconciles with the existing DescentEvidence and measurement bases rather than being minted as a third confidence vocabulary. Migrating the axis is owed; minting the enum is not." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data concrete_input_evaluation: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: ConcreteInputEvaluation },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "eval_cost_expr_concrete"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: DiscreteCostC1Valuation,
    note: "CENSUS CORRECTION to the sibling note's correction 1, which said no environment binding a size variable to a value existed anywhere in the tree. The seed has one: eval_size_expr_concrete and eval_cost_expr_concrete take env: Map<String, Int> and return Int?, refusing with Absent on CostUnknown / CostExtern / CostLog rather than fabricating a number. The accurate claim is narrower and more useful — the seam is absent from V2, and v1's is String-keyed with a limited evaluation and refusal surface, so C1 inherits a refusal posture to preserve and a key to re-ground rather than a blank page." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data interprocedural_summary_memo: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: InterproceduralSummaryMemo },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "get_or_compute_summary"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "CostInternTable memoizes a summary per function so it is reused across call sites; its Map<String, ComplexitySummary> re-grounds onto declaration-keyed identity. See SummaryEviction for the fail-open arm that must not migrate with it." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data summary_eviction: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: LegacyHazardItem { hazard: SummaryEvictionHazard, of_capability: InterproceduralSummaryMemo },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "evict_summary"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "CENSUS HAZARD. evict_summary does not remove an entry — it overwrites it with a zero-work, zero-span, Proven summary, so a later lookup on an evicted function returns 'costs nothing, proven' rather than 'not computed'. That is the state-space conflation this repository names and the zero-for-unmodelled arm the sibling note's honesty states forbid. The eviction capability may migrate; this realization may not. The migrated form removes the entry or refuses, and the discriminating control executes the seed behavior rather than matching its source text." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data call_graph_and_scc: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: CallGraphAndScc },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "build_scc_index"),
  disposition: IntegrationOwed {
    substrate_carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "std.graph", decl_name: "graph_multi_node_scc_members"),
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "CENSUS CORRECTION to the parity table, which read 'Call graph / SCC — v1 yes, v2 no' and would have made this migration work. std.graph already carries CallGraph, forward and reverse adjacency, dfs_finish_order, dfs_collect_component, graph_multi_node_scc_members, graph_cycle_members and is_valid_proof, and v1.compiler.complexity reaches them by import; v2's lens modules import std.graph nowhere. Corrected again on operator review: the work is INTEGRATION, not nothing. Calling it discharged understated C4 — v2 must still consume the substrate and produce the facts it consumes." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data descent_evidence_vocabulary: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: DescentEvidenceVocabulary },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "construct_termination_proof"),
  disposition: IntegrationOwed {
    substrate_carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "std.termination", decl_name: "DescentEvidence"),
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "std.termination owns DescentEvidence, RankingDimension, DescentSource, TerminationProof and ProofEdge with the lattice meet and evidence rank, all imported by v1.compiler.complexity. The vocabulary a recursion bound is stated in is already consolidated on its single authority; what remains is v2 producing and consuming those facts, tracked with the walker row separately." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data cost_bound_vocabulary: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: CostBoundVocabulary },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "extract_shrink_factor"),
  disposition: IntegrationOwed {
    substrate_carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "std.induction", decl_name: "derive_bound"),
    phase: ParityC1ExpressionAuthority,
    note: "std.induction carries SubValueRelation, CostBound with its Constant/Atomic/Forever/Error arms, AtomicCost, PolyCost, catamorphism_bound and derive_bound — a bound vocabulary derived from sub-value structure, imported by the seed. Named so C1 does not re-mint a cost-bound taxonomy the substrate already carries; the sibling note's do-not-re-mint list did not include it and now does." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data lowering_target_vocabulary: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: LoweringTargetVocabulary },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "proof_to_call_pattern"),
  disposition: IntegrationOwed {
    substrate_carrier: decl_ref(module_path: "std.computation", decl_name: "lower_call_pattern"),
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "std.computation owns CallPattern, LoweringTarget, lower_call_pattern, size_bound_param, IterationDimension and ShrinkFactor, all imported by the seed. The recursion-shape vocabulary a bounded recursive cost is computed against is shared substrate awaiting a v2 consumer." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data descent_derivation_walkers: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: DescentDerivationWalkers },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "collect_descent_vars"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "What is genuinely seed-local: the walkers that PRODUCE the shared vocabulary by traversing the v1 AST — collect_descent_vars, classify_self_call_evidence, all_self_calls_descend and their neighbours, each threading a source index. They do not port; they are re-expressed as a fold over the closed v2 Node kernel, which is why the ruling says not to port the seed module wholesale. With the integration rows above discharging the vocabulary, C4's real surface is this derivation layer plus the interprocedural memo." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data recursion_pattern_classification: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: RecursionPatternClassification },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "classify_recursion_pattern"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromCostLens,
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "Selects a LoweringTarget from a function body and its termination proof, against which bounded_recursive_cost charges the per-iteration cost. The target vocabulary is shared; the classifier is a v1-AST walker and migrates with the derivation layer." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data parser_progress_fact_production: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: SemanticCapabilityItem { capability: ParserProgressFactProduction },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "collect_parser_progress_edges"),
  disposition: MigrationOwed {
    destination: MigrateToV2LensCost,
    producer: FactsFromV2Parser,
    phase: ParityC4Interprocedural,
    note: "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-05 — the semantic capability SURVIVES: parser recursion advances token position, cycles carry strict descent, and depth derives from input. Those are facts a v2 parser plus std.termination integration must produce. This row replaces the previous DispositionDeferred row, which the ruling resolved; deferral is no longer a disposition this census can express." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data parser_progress_v1_walker: V1ComplexityInventoryRow = V1ComplexityInventoryRow {
  item: LegacyRealizationItem { realization: ParserProgressV1WalkerRealization, of_capability: ParserProgressFactProduction },
  v1_carrier: v1_ref(decl_name: "parser_state_expr_progress"),
  disposition: RetirementOwed {
    trigger: RetiresWhenReplacementExecutes { replacement: ParserProgressFactProduction },
    note: "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-05 — the v1 AST and source-index walker realization does NOT survive. It is not retired yet: retiring before the replacement executes would delete a live capability, so the obligation is owed and the trigger names the capability whose execution discharges it. This is the arm the previous revision could not express — the old DeliberatelyRetired arm belonged to no work-state partition, so exhaustiveness was green only because nothing inhabited it." as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

